# Graph-Works Workspace

> **Topic:** `{{TOPIC}}`
> **Initialized:** `{{INITIALIZED_AT}}`
> **Bundle dir:** `{{BUNDLE_DIR}}/`
> **Config dir:** `{{CONFIG_DIR}}/`

This is a graph-works workspace: a per-repo container for the knowledge graph, its
config, and its work tracker. `{{BUNDLE_DIR}}/` is the OKF v0.2 bundle (the content
itself); `{{CONFIG_DIR}}/` holds everything graph-works owns about it. The workspace
is strictly larger than the bundle: `{{CONFIG_DIR}}/` and `scratch/` are siblings of
the bundle root, not bundle content.

The repositories this workspace catalogs are declared in `workspace.yaml` under
`repositories:`, with paths relative to that file. Nothing in this document names an
absolute path: machine-local locations come from the `gw` CLI's own discovery and
from the skill-doc-routing hook, never from here.

## Layout

- `workspace.yaml` — the workspace manifest, committed. `layout.bundle_dir` (here:
  `{{BUNDLE_DIR}}`), the scanned `repositories:` and their `ignore:` globs, and the
  `workflow:` block that configures auto-drive (per-phase models, `max_parallel`, the
  seeded relay tail).
- `workspace.local.yaml` — an optional, gitignored sibling of `workspace.yaml` for
  per-machine values: a `repositories.<name>.path` that differs on this machine,
  `workflow.auto_drive` settings that suit this box. Mappings deep-merge over
  `workspace.yaml`; lists and scalars replace; local wins. Born by
  `gw config set --local <key> <value>`; `gw config get` / `gw config list` report
  which layer a value came from. `gw bootstrap` keeps its line in the workspace-root
  `.gitignore`.
- `{{BUNDLE_DIR}}/` — the OKF bundle: `index.md`, `log.md`, and every page the
  installed verticals write. See "The bundle" below.
- `{{CONFIG_DIR}}/schema/` — per-type JSON Schema (`Bug.schema.json`,
  `Package.schema.json`, …) plus the shared `_base.schema.json` /
  `_base-diataxis.schema.json`. Defines the frontmatter each `type:` requires.
- `{{CONFIG_DIR}}/sections/` — per-type section declarations (required headings and
  their placeholders), plus the root-index sections each vertical owns and the shared
  `_fragments.*.yaml`.
- `{{CONFIG_DIR}}/tags.yaml` — the bundle's tag vocabulary. One name + description per
  tag; the work lane and curated pages draw `tags:` from it.
- `{{CONFIG_DIR}}/cache/` — derived and disposable: the code graph (`code.db`), scan
  results, traces, and the `config.json` projection of the manifest. Gitignored.
- `{{CONFIG_DIR}}/worktrees/` — feature-work worktrees created under this workspace.
  Gitignored.
- `scratch/` — handoffs, staged material, and scratch documents. Not bundle content.
  Ingest reads material from anywhere on disk, so nothing has to be staged here first.

## The `gw` CLI

Everything is driven through `gw`; the workspace is discovered automatically
(`GRAPH_WORKS_DIR`, else `<repo>/.works`). `gw <cmd> --help` is authoritative. The
top-level verbs:

| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `gw bootstrap` | Initialize or re-render this workspace (regenerates this file) |
| `gw scan` | Build the code graph and write one page per admitted entity |
| `gw ingest <path>` | Ingest one source into the bundle |
| `gw query "<q>"` | Answer one question against the bundle |
| `gw next <path>` | Compute what to dispatch for a work item |
| `gw archive` | Sweep eligible work items and proposals into `_archive/` |
| `gw wiki …` | `lint`, `stats`, `index`, `archive`, `proposals`, `proposal` |
| `gw work …` | `file`, `status`, `next`, `advance`, `lint`, `regen-index`, `orchestrate`, `archive`, `reparent`, `adopt`, `decision` |
| `gw config …` | `get` / `set` / `list` / `unset` / `sync` / `hooks` over the manifest |
| `gw graph …` | Code-graph queries |
| `gw util …` | Diagnostics: log, tokens, traces, surface freeze |

Prefer the `/gw:*` skills for interactive work — they wrap these verbs with the
discussion and confirmation steps the raw CLI skips.

## The bundle

`{{BUNDLE_DIR}}/` **is** the bundle root. Every root-absolute markdown link — such as
`[Work Index](/work/index.md)` — resolves relative to it, not to the workspace and not
to the filesystem root.

```
<workspace>/
├── workspace.yaml            manifest (committed)
├── workspace.local.yaml      per-machine overrides (gitignored, optional)
├── AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md      this file and its one-line pointer
├── scratch/                  handoffs; not bundle content
├── {{CONFIG_DIR}}/           schema/, sections/, tags.yaml, cache/, worktrees/
└── {{BUNDLE_DIR}}/           the bundle; the three lanes own everything inside
    ├── index.md, log.md      root index and the append-only timeline
    ├── repositories/ dependencies/
    ├── explanations/ references/ tutorials/ how-tos/
    ├── sources/ adrs/ proposals/
    └── work/
```

There is no `raw/` and no `entities/`; both belonged to the legacy graph-wiki layout.
Material is ingested from anywhere on disk, and entity pages live in per-type lanes.

Three verticals write into this one bundle. Each owns its directories outright.

### Code wiki — `code_wiki_okf`, written by `gw scan`

Graph-derived. One page per admitted code entity, regenerated from the code graph.
**Do not hand-author these** — change the code or the scan config, then rescan.

| Directory | Type | Filename |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `repositories/<repo>/` | `Repository` | `repository.md` |
| `repositories/<repo>/packages/` | `Package` | `<name>.md` |
| `repositories/<repo>/apps/` | `App` | `<name>.md` |
| `repositories/<repo>/agent-plugins/` | `AgentPlugin` | `<name>.md` |
| `repositories/<repo>/test-suites/` | `TestSuite` | `<name>.md` |
| `repositories/<repo>/files/` | `File` | `<repo-relative-path>.md` |
| `dependencies/` | `Dependency` | `<ecosystem>/<name>.md` |

Entity pages are nested under their owning repository, one folder per kind, so a
repository page reaches its members by relative path. `dependencies/` is the one
sibling root, because a dependency belongs to no single repository. There are no
top-level `packages/`, `apps/`, `agent-plugins/` or `test-suites/` directories.

Generated pages carry a `generated:` block (`by:`, `at:`) and a `resource:` URI such as
`dependency:pypi/pytest`. Prose sections under the frontmatter (`## Why we depend on
this`, `## Gotchas / workarounds`) are human- or agent-authored and **survive rescan** —
that is where hard-won knowledge belongs.

### Doc wiki — `doc_wiki_okf`

The four Diátaxis lanes plus the material they are drawn from:

| Directory | Type | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `explanations/` | `Explanation` | Understanding-oriented: why a thing is shaped this way |
| `references/` | `Reference` | Information-oriented: what is true of a thing |
| `how-tos/` | `HowTo` | Goal-oriented: solve one problem |
| `tutorials/` | `Tutorial` | Learning-oriented: build one thing end to end |
| `sources/` | `Source` | One summary per ingested source, at `sources/<YYYY-MM>-<slug>.md` |
| `adrs/` | `Adr` | Architecture decision records, `<NNNN>-<slug>.md` |
| `proposals/` | `Proposal` | Staged curated pages awaiting disposition |

A proposal names its `target:` (e.g. `adrs/bulk-write-staging-protocol.md`) and cites
the `sources:` that justify it. `/gw:proposals` accepts, rejects, or supersedes them;
accepting authors the destination page and archives the proposal.

### Work tracker — `work_tracker_okf`

Path-native work items. Each item is a markdown file with a **sibling directory of the
same name**:

```
work/bug-csharp-scanning-import-defects.md          the item itself
work/bug-csharp-scanning-import-defects/
├── references/
│   ├── 00-decisions.md                             decision ledger
│   ├── 01-design.md                                design-stage artifact
│   ├── 02-plan.md                                  plan-stage artifact
│   └── index.md
└── children/                                       sub-items, same shape, recursively
```

The canonical path of an item is its **extensionless bundle-relative path** —
`work/epic-auto-drive-dispatch-correctness/children/bug-at-end-commit-no-plan`. That is
what every `gw work` verb takes and what links should point at (with `.md` appended).

Types: `Bug`, `Feature`, `TechDebt`, `Spike`, `TestGap`, `Epic`, `Release`. Epics and
Releases roll up children rather than carrying their own implementation.

`work/index.md` is the generated index — reconcile it with `gw work regen-index` after
any out-of-band edit. Terminal items sweep to `work/_archive/` via `gw work archive`.

## The pipeline

A work item advances through phases; `gw work next <path>` computes the stage and the
skill to dispatch, and `gw work advance <path>` applies the transition.

```
design → plan → execute → finish → done
```

`gw work next --json` returns the `action.skill` to run, the `artifact.path` it must
write, and the `on_dispatch` / `on_complete` frontmatter deltas. Dispatch one stage per
session with `/gw:workflow <path>` and clear context between stages. `--descend`
switches to the next actionable child when a parent is waiting on its subtree.

Frontmatter carries the live state: `phase`, `work_status`, `worktree`, `branch`,
`affects`, `depends_on`, `sources`.

Unattended runs go through `/gw:auto-drive`, which dispatches each ready stage as an
Orca-supervised worker using the per-phase models in `workspace.yaml`. Re-running the
same command is the resume path.

## Frontmatter

Every page opens with YAML frontmatter. `type:` selects the contract:

```yaml
---
type: <Bug | Feature | Package | Explanation | Adr | Source | …>
title: <Title>
description: <one-line summary>
tags: [tag1, tag2]
updated: YYYY-MM-DD
---
```

`{{CONFIG_DIR}}/schema/<Type>.schema.json` is authoritative for required fields, and
`{{CONFIG_DIR}}/sections/<Type>.yaml` for required headings and their placeholder text.
Read those rather than copying another page's shape — older pages may still carry
legacy keys (`category:`, `kind:`) from the graph-wiki era.

`tags:` must come from the vocabulary in `{{CONFIG_DIR}}/tags.yaml`. The work lane does
not enforce the vocabulary; curated lanes do.

## The four operations

Each has a `/gw:*` skill that adds discussion and confirmation around the CLI verb. Use
the skill interactively; use the verb when scripting.

### Scan — `/gw:scan` → `gw scan`

Walks the repositories in `workspace.yaml`, rebuilds the code graph, and writes one page
per admitted entity. Reports created / updated / deleted by URI and surfaces deletions
for confirmation. Regenerates the affected indexes and appends a `scan` log entry.

### Ingest — `/gw:ingest <path>` → `gw ingest <path>`

1. Read the source directly (any path on disk, or a repo-relative `.md` design doc)
2. **Discuss first** — TL;DR, key claims, which entities and concepts get touched, any
   contradictions with the code
3. Wait for confirmation
4. Write the summary at `sources/<YYYY-MM>-<slug>.md`
5. Update every relevant entity, doc, and ADR page (typically 5–15)
6. Propose an ADR if the source records a decision; file curated pages as proposals
7. Flag contradictions with `> ⚠️ Contradiction:` callouts
8. Update the indexes, append an `ingest` log entry, report touched pages as links

### Query — `/gw:query "<question>"` → `gw query`

Read `index.md` first, pick 3–10 relevant pages across lanes, read them in full, follow
links opportunistically, fall back to the code when the bundle does not cover it.
Synthesize: direct answer → supporting detail → inline links and `` `code-path:line` ``
→ related pages. **Offer to file the answer back** as a proposal.

### Lint — `/gw:lint` → `gw wiki lint` / `gw work lint`

Mechanical checks (orphans, broken links, missing frontmatter, duplicate titles, index
drift, log gaps), code drift (what is on disk vs. what the bundle claims), semantic
checks (contradictions, stale claims, ADR chain health), and the work-lane conformance
rules. Present findings as a report with suggested actions; append a `lint` entry.

## Iron rules

1. **The code is the source of truth.** If the bundle and the code disagree, update the
   bundle — never the other way around.
2. **Links are root-absolute markdown links**, not `[[wikilinks]]`. `okf_io.LinkGraph`
   parses markdown links and cannot see a wikilink at all.
3. **All bundle writes go under `{{BUNDLE_DIR}}/`** — work items included.
4. **Generated pages are regenerated, not hand-edited.** Prose sections beneath the
   generated frontmatter are yours; the frontmatter is the scanner's.
5. **Every page has frontmatter** conforming to its `type:`'s schema.
6. **Every claim cites** — a bundle page (`[…](/sources/….md)`) or a code path
   (`` `packages/okf-io/src/okf_io/bundle.py:42` ``).
7. **Contradictions get flagged inline**, on the page and against the code path.
8. **Good query answers get filed back.** Explorations compound.
9. **Indexes are reconciled, not written by hand** — `gw wiki index`, `gw work
   regen-index`.
10. **Never hand-edit `{{CONFIG_DIR}}/cache/`** — regenerate it.

## Log format

Appended to `{{BUNDLE_DIR}}/log.md`. One `## <ISO date>` section per day, newest first;
one list item per entry (OKF v0.2 §9). `okf_io.log` enforces both: a non-ISO heading is
reported as `reserved.log-heading-not-date`, and non-newest-first ordering is reported
too.

```
## YYYY-MM-DD

- **<Op>** <title> — <optional detail: which pages touched, what changed>
```

Valid ops: `scan`, `ingest`, `query`, `lint`, `create`, `update`, `delete`, `note`.

Grep the log: `grep -E "^## [0-9]{4}-" {{BUNDLE_DIR}}/log.md | tail -10`

## Style

- Be concise. Wiki pages are read, not generated.
- Prefer short paragraphs. Bulleted lists where appropriate.
- Cite aggressively with markdown links and `` `code-path:line` ``.
- When you're not sure, say so in the page. Don't invent content.
- Update `updated:` frontmatter whenever you touch a page.

